   "id": "sec-foundations",
   "kind": "section",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "Foundations",
    "order": 1
   },
   "body": "The basic vocabulary: covers, filters, nets, and the definition of compactness we will actually use.\n\nEverything in this section is elementary; the point is to fix the formulation that makes [thm-tychonoff] painless.",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/foundations/_section.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "sec-main",
   "kind": "section",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "The main theorem",
    "order": 2
   },
   "body": "We prove Tychonoff's theorem for arbitrary products. The argument is the ultrafilter one; the classical subcover argument is recorded in [sec-main-induction] because it is what the applications actually use.",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/main/_section.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "sec-main-induction",
   "kind": "section",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "The subcover induction",
    "order": 1
   },
   "body": "A self-contained induction on the number of factors. It is logically redundant given [lem-ultrafilter] but it is constructive and it is what [sec-applications] reuses.",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/main/induction/_section.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "sec-applications",
   "kind": "section",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "Applications",
    "order": 3
   },
   "body": "Heine–Borel and the Stone–Čech compactification, as consumers of the main theorem.",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/applications/_section.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "def-compact",
   "kind": "definition",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "Compact space",
    "lean": [
     "Compact.isCompact"
    ],
    "review": "reviewed",
    "tags": [
     "core"
    ],
    "order": 1
   },
   "body": "A space $X$ is **compact** when every open cover of $X$ admits a finite subcover:\n\n$$\\forall\\, \\mathcal{U} \\subseteq \\mathrm{Open}(X),\\quad X = \\bigcup \\mathcal{U} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\exists\\, F \\subseteq \\mathcal{U} \\text{ finite},\\ X = \\bigcup F.$$\n\nWe do not assume Hausdorff. The filter-theoretic reformulation is [def-filter].",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/foundations/def-compact.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "def-filter",
   "kind": "definition",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "Filters and ultrafilters",
    "lean": [
     "Compact.Filter",
     "Compact.Ultrafilter"
    ],
    "review": "ready",
    "tags": [
     "core"
    ],
    "order": 2
   },
   "body": "A **filter** on $X$ is a nonempty upward-closed set $\\mathcal{F} \\subseteq \\mathcal{P}(X)$ closed under finite intersection with $\\emptyset \\notin \\mathcal{F}$. An **ultrafilter** is a maximal filter; equivalently $A \\in \\mathcal{F}$ or $X \\setminus A \\in \\mathcal{F}$ for every $A$.\n\nCompare [def-compact]: $X$ is compact iff every ultrafilter on $X$ converges.",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/foundations/def-filter.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "def-net",
   "kind": "definition",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "Nets and subnets",
    "lean": [
     "Compact.Net"
    ],
    "review": "draft",
    "order": 3
   },
   "body": "A **net** in $X$ is a function from a directed set to $X$. Nets and filters carry the same information; we keep both because the limit-point argument reads better with nets.",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/foundations/def-net.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "def-metric",
   "kind": "definition",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "Metric spaces",
    "review": "draft",
    "order": 4
   },
   "body": "The usual $(X, d)$ with $d$ symmetric, separating and satisfying the triangle inequality. Not yet formalised: this object carries no `lean` attribute, so its derived status is `absent`.",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/foundations/def-metric.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "lem-ultrafilter",
   "kind": "lemma",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "Ultrafilter criterion",
    "lean": [
     "Compact.ultrafilter_le"
    ],
    "review": "reviewed",
    "tags": [
     "core",
     "choice"
    ],
    "order": 5
   },
   "body": "$X$ is compact if and only if every ultrafilter on $X$ converges to some point of $X$.\n\nThe proof uses the ultrafilter lemma, hence the derived status `proved_with_axioms`: it depends on `Classical.choice` plus a project-local axiom.",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/foundations/lem-ultrafilter.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "thm-tychonoff",
   "kind": "theorem",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "Tychonoff's theorem",
    "lean": [
     "Compact.tychonoff"
    ],
    "review": "ready",
    "tags": [
     "headline"
    ],
    "order": 1
   },
   "body": "Let $(X_i)_{i \\in I}$ be a family of compact spaces. Then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is compact in the product topology.\n\nThe proof is [uses/thm-tychonoff/lem-ultrafilter]: push an ultrafilter forward along each projection, take a limit coordinatewise, and check that the resulting point is a limit of the original ultrafilter.",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/main/thm-tychonoff.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "lem-finite-subcover",
   "kind": "lemma",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "Finite products, by subcovers",
    "lean": [
     "Compact.finite_subcover"
    ],
    "review": "ready",
    "order": 1
   },
   "body": "If $X$ and $Y$ are compact then so is $X \\times Y$, by the tube lemma. Induction gives finite products without any choice.",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/main/induction/lem-finite-subcover.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "lem-diagonal",
   "kind": "lemma",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "Diagonal extraction",
    "lean": [
     "Compact.diagonal"
    ],
    "review": "ready",
    "tags": [
     "shared"
    ],
    "order": 2
   },
   "body": "A countable product of sequentially compact spaces is sequentially compact.\n\nThis lemma genuinely belongs to two sections, so it has two `refines` parents and is flagged by the `multi-parent` lint.",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/main/induction/lem-diagonal.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "lem-base-case",
   "kind": "lemma",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "Base case: a single factor",
    "lean": [
     "Compact.baseCase"
    ],
    "review": "reviewed",
    "order": 1
   },
   "body": "For $|I| = 1$ the product is homeomorphic to $X_{i_0}$ and there is nothing to prove. The ultrafilter pushforward is the identity under this homeomorphism; see [def-filter].",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/main/uses-tychonoff-ultrafilter/lem-base-case.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "lem-inductive-step",
   "kind": "lemma",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "Coordinatewise limits exist",
    "lean": [
     "Compact.inductiveStep"
    ],
    "review": "draft",
    "order": 2
   },
   "body": "For each $i$, the pushforward $\\pi_{i*}\\mathcal{U}$ is an ultrafilter on the compact space $X_i$, hence converges to some $x_i$. Choice of the $x_i$ is where the axiom enters.",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/main/uses-tychonoff-ultrafilter/lem-inductive-step.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "lem-limit-point",
   "kind": "lemma",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "The candidate point is a limit",
    "lean": [
     "Compact.limitPoint"
    ],
    "review": "draft",
    "order": 3
   },
   "body": "Let $x = (x_i)_i$. A basic open neighbourhood of $x$ constrains finitely many coordinates, so it lies in $\\mathcal{U}$; hence $\\mathcal{U} \\to x$ and the product is compact by [lem-ultrafilter].",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/main/uses-tychonoff-ultrafilter/lem-limit-point.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "thm-heine-borel",
   "kind": "theorem",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "Heine–Borel",
    "lean": [
     "Compact.heineBorel"
    ],
    "review": "draft",
    "tags": [
     "application"
    ],
    "order": 1
   },
   "body": "A subset of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is compact if and only if it is closed and bounded.\n\nThe `lean` attribute names `Compact.heineBorel`, which does not exist in the environment, so the derived status is `missing`.",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/applications/thm-heine-borel.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "thm-stone-cech",
   "kind": "theorem",
   "boundary": [],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "Stone–Čech compactification",
    "review": "draft",
    "tags": [
     "application"
    ],
    "order": 2
   },
   "body": "Every Tychonoff space $X$ embeds in a compact Hausdorff $\\beta X$ with the universal property\n\n$$\\mathrm{Hom}(\\beta X, K) \\cong \\mathrm{Hom}(X, K) \\quad (K \\text{ compact Hausdorff}).$$\n\nNot started. The intended construction goes through [def-uniformity], which does not exist yet — the `bad-link` lint catches it.",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/applications/thm-stone-cech.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "uses/thm-tychonoff/lem-ultrafilter",
   "kind": "uses",
   "boundary": [
    {
     "role": "src",
     "id": "thm-tychonoff"
    },
    {
     "role": "tgt",
     "id": "lem-ultrafilter"
    }
   ],
   "attrs": {
    "title": "Ultrafilter proof of Tychonoff"
   },
   "body": "Let $\\mathcal{U}$ be an ultrafilter on $\\prod_i X_i$. The argument has three steps: [lem-base-case], [lem-inductive-step] and [lem-limit-point].\n\nThis edge carries prose and is itself refined by those three lemmas, so it is an object that can be expanded like any other.",
   "source": {
    "file": "blueprint/main/uses-tychonoff-ultrafilter.md",
    "anonymous": false
   },
   "depth": 1
